VeridianOS: fix ksystemstats warnings (unchecked opens, uninitialised meminfo, dead stores)

GCC reported:
- -Wunused-result on QFile::open() of /proc/diskstats (disks.cpp),
  /proc/cpuinfo and /proc/stat (linuxcpuplugin.cpp) and /proc/meminfo
  (memory/linuxbackend.cpp). A failed open is now handled: the disk, cpu
  and memory update() return early (same as the existing "file does not
  exist" path in disks.cpp), the cpu constructor logs with
  KSYSTEMSTATS_CPU and carries on so m_allCpus is still created.
- -Wmaybe-uninitialized on all eight counters in
  LinuxMemoryBackend::update(). Real bug: any field missing from
  /proc/meminfo (MemAvailable before Linux 3.14, or a minimal procfs such
  as VeridianOS's) left the variable uninitialised and its stack garbage
  was published as a sensor value. They now start at 0.
- -Wunused-but-set-variable 'tccd' in addSensorsAmd(). The array was only
  written, never read (the CCD mapping is still a TODO). It was also a real
  out-of-bounds write: it is indexed by the number in the hwmon feature
  name ("temp3".."temp10" for Tccd1..Tccd8), so Tccd6..Tccd8 wrote
  tccd[8..10] past the end of the 8-element stack array. The dead array is
  removed; Tccd sensors are still recognised (no "unrecognised sensor"
  warning), as before.
- -Wunused-but-set-variable 'gpuCounter' in NvidiaSmiProcess::query():
  counted but never read; removed.

Behaviour is otherwise unchanged.

